1
00:00:02,276 --> 00:00:04,232
Now that we had a look at Arrays, Sets,

2
00:00:04,584 --> 00:00:07,165
Objects, and Maps, we got all the basics

3
00:00:07,165 --> 00:00:10,276
that we need for this course to finally also

4
00:00:10,292 --> 00:00:13,017
dive into custom data structures. However,

5
00:00:13,033 --> 00:00:16,432
not before I at least also briefly touch on

6
00:00:16,624 --> 00:00:19,189
WeakSets and WeakMaps. We learned about Set

7
00:00:19,189 --> 00:00:19,654
and Map.

8
00:00:20,215 --> 00:00:23,277
WeakSet and WeakMap are simply variations of

9
00:00:23,277 --> 00:00:24,159
Set and Map.

10
00:00:25,104 --> 00:00:27,718
The difference to Set and Map is that in the

11
00:00:27,766 --> 00:00:30,956
WeakSet and the WeakMap, the values, in the

12
00:00:30,956 --> 00:00:33,604
case of the WeakSet, and the keys in the

13
00:00:33,604 --> 00:00:36,476
case of the WeakMap are only weakly

14
00:00:36,733 --> 00:00:37,407
referenced.

15
00:00:38,113 --> 00:00:39,589
Now that's an abstract term.

16
00:00:40,344 --> 00:00:42,815
It simply means that the JavaScript garbage

17
00:00:42,911 --> 00:00:45,318
collection, which is part of the JavaScript

18
00:00:45,334 --> 00:00:47,934
engine, part of the browser, for example, is

19
00:00:48,127 --> 00:00:51,336
able to actually delete values or keys and

20
00:00:51,432 --> 00:00:52,090
their values

21
00:00:52,861 --> 00:00:56,231
if those values or keys are not referenced

22
00:00:56,391 --> 00:00:58,556
anywhere else in your app. So if you, for

23
00:00:58,556 --> 00:01:00,992
example, have a Set and you got a bunch of

24
00:01:00,992 --> 00:01:01,729
IDs in there

25
00:01:02,402 --> 00:01:05,269
and JavaScript finds out that nowhere else

26
00:01:05,526 --> 00:01:07,625
in your code you're using that value

27
00:01:07,689 --> 00:01:10,845
anymore, in such a case, it frees up that

28
00:01:10,845 --> 00:01:13,440
space in memory. And therefore, WeakSets and

29
00:01:13,472 --> 00:01:16,324
WeakMaps can give you an extra memory

30
00:01:16,420 --> 00:01:19,881
advantage because unused space can be

31
00:01:19,945 --> 00:01:20,522
cleared up.

32
00:01:21,083 --> 00:01:23,678
In a traditional Set or Map and also in

33
00:01:23,726 --> 00:01:26,594
traditional Arrays and Objects, such space

34
00:01:26,626 --> 00:01:27,587
is not cleared up.

35
00:01:28,116 --> 00:01:31,226
The values are kept around forever as long

36
00:01:31,338 --> 00:01:33,550
as your array and object exists, basically,

37
00:01:33,903 --> 00:01:36,002
no matter if you still use a value in an

38
00:01:36,002 --> 00:01:36,692
array or not.

39
00:01:37,429 --> 00:01:40,458
Now again, in a lot of scenarios this won't

40
00:01:40,539 --> 00:01:42,318
matter, but if you have a really

41
00:01:42,398 --> 00:01:43,712
performance-intensive

42
00:01:44,386 --> 00:01:46,982
application that uses a lot of memory, if

43
00:01:47,030 --> 00:01:48,810
you're building your own framework and you

44
00:01:48,810 --> 00:01:51,262
need every piece of optimization, then

45
00:01:51,679 --> 00:01:54,115
WeakSet and WeakMap could help you with

46
00:01:54,179 --> 00:01:56,716
memory management. Now, we won't need them

47
00:01:56,764 --> 00:01:59,241
in the course here and arguably you won't

48
00:01:59,241 --> 00:02:00,800
need them in a lot of applications you're

49
00:02:00,849 --> 00:02:03,469
going to build probably, but still it is

50
00:02:03,582 --> 00:02:05,913
worth knowing them and since we talk about

51
00:02:05,913 --> 00:02:08,309
data structures here, I certainly can't move

52
00:02:08,309 --> 00:02:10,062
on without at least mentioning them.

53
00:02:10,802 --> 00:02:13,117
With that, however, let's finally dive into

54
00:02:13,149 --> 00:02:14,468
a custom data structure.
